+\begin{abstract}
+A cyclic change of disk coordinate turns a finite monodromy into a
+smooth central fibre. The generator that rotates the new disk positively
+acts on this fibre by the inverse of positive transport. We derive this
+inverse directly for an explicit Weierstrass family and retain the full
+integral period lattice of a four-torus. Two affine disk quotients then
+make the effect on boundary chains explicit. For each quotient we give
+its smooth disk trivialization, fibre covering, oriented boundary map,
+and finite integral chains in all degrees. The correctly directed
+transport determines a positive prism homotopy and the complete boundary
+projection. The affine descent is determined from the conormal coordinate of the
+origin section in the Weierstrass reduction. Its nonzero central
+translation and the winding of the original section combine in an
+explicit integral change of fibre coordinates. This gives the marked
+local filling and its positive boundary maps, under the stated smooth
+line-bundle reduction hypotheses. Distinct affine descents show why
+linear monodromy alone would not suffice.
+\end{abstract}
